From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 03:20:56 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] Embedding: compute x86's SIMD floating-point results

An embedding host wants the guest's floating point to be x86's on any
host, and the arm64 operations differ from x86's in places. A new IR
op, EmbedderFPCheck, takes the host's result of an x86 SIMD
floating-point instruction, or of one 128-bit part of it, with the
instruction's sources in x86's order and a descriptor of the
operation. Where the host's result can differ from x86's, the op
branches to a cold site after the block, which hands the values to the
host through Pointers.EmbedderFPHelper in Embedder.FPScratch, from a
shared stub that preserves registers as the TLB-miss stub does; the
helper returns x86's result there, or records an exit that leaves the
dispatch before the instruction wrote anything. Register allocation
treats the op as one that can leave the block.

The arithmetic, square root, horizontal, ADDSUB, DPPS, DPPD and FMA
operations, SSE and VEX, take the cold path for a NaN result: x86
propagates the first source's NaN, keeps the sign of a NaN it
subtracts or negates, and generates the negative indefinite, and a DPPS
sums each lane in its own order, which picks among NaNs per lane. The
op takes the x86 sources before FEXCore rearranges them: an FMA's a, b
and c in x86's order, and ADDSUB's and FMADDSUB's addend before the
sign flip.

A context translates for one floating-point mode, MXCSR.DAZ and FTZ,
which the host sets with SetEmbedderFPMode before InitCore. LDMXCSR,
FXRSTOR and XRSTOR end with EmbedderFPModeCheck, which leaves the
dispatch after the instruction with EMBEDDER_EXIT_MODE when the MXCSR
it stored has another mode, so that the host runs the rest in that
mode's context. Embedded, LDMXCSR sets FPCR.FZ from DAZ rather than
FTZ: FZ flushes inputs as DAZ does, and the checks apply FTZ, which
x86 does after rounding where FZ flushes a result that is tiny before
it. Under DAZ and FTZ a sum tiny enough to flush is exact, so a
product, quotient, FMA or CVTSD2SS/CVTPD2PS takes the cold path only
for a zero from nonzero inputs, found out of line after an in-line
test for any zero; a DPPS or DPPD checks its products as well. Under
FTZ alone a result up to the smallest normal takes it, and under DAZ
alone a zero result. MINPS and MAXPS under DAZ take it for a denormal
result, which FZ compares flushed but returns as it was, and a DPPS or
DPPD under DAZ or FTZ alone, which rounds in steps the result does not
show, always takes it.

RCPPS, RCPSS, RSQRTPS and RSQRTSS, and their VEX forms, go through the
op instead of FRECPE and FRSQRTE, whose results depend on FEAT_RPRES
and differ from x86's: x86 takes a denormal input as zero, returns a
zero for the reciprocal of 2^126 or more and the indefinite NaN for
the reciprocal square root of a negative number. The op computes the
correctly rounded quotient in line, which lies within x86's error,
and sends the inputs needing more to the host.
